          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Lorck\",\"addressee\":\"Constance\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Lorck directly presents his love for Constance as a true and serious emotional state. This truth-presenting avowal is the focal expression's clearest and dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.82,\"reason\":\"Within the integrated appeal, Lorck's declaration affirms a seriously committed stance toward Constance and introduces an offer of his whole self, life, and soul. Thus the occurrence has moderate undertaking force as part of a proposed bond. The explicit enactment of the offer, however, is in the following clause rather than wholly contained in “Jeg elsker dig,” so commitment is not its dominant force. Constance refuses the offer.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Nej, uf nej,\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Although the scene is emotionally intense, this occurrence is a deliberate avowal and appeal, explicitly characterized as serious and followed by a request for acceptance. The evidence does not present it as a reflex-like or involuntary verbal discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"det er Alvor\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"vil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"No substantial residual force is needed beyond avowal and the contextually integrated relational undertaking. The persuasive courtship function is adequately accounted for by T and P.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Constance resists Lorck's advances and he presses her to say whether she is dismissing him, Lorck addresses her directly, renews his declaration of love, insists on its seriousness, and offers her himself, his life, and his soul. Constance immediately rejects the offer and withdraws.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Lorck offers his whole self, life, and soul to Constance, positioning himself as willing to enter a total but unspecified relational bond. The focal declaration participates in that integrated appeal by affirming the serious loving stance on which the offer rests, although the explicit self-giving is a distinct adjoining clause. Constance gives clear negative uptake: “Nej, uf nej,” and later tells him he should leave.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognize the characters and passage as likely coming from Amalie Skram's novel “Constance Ring.” The classification relies only on the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents Lorck as passionately affected, but it cannot establish his inner sincerity independently of the literary narration. The practical terms and duration of his proposed self-giving are unspecified. No claims are made about omitted context or later events.\"}"
        }
